    "slug": "un-political-mission-in-somalia-concludes-after-30-years",
    "summary": "The United Nations is ending its political mission in Somalia after more than three decades, as the country continues to face political divisions, an Islamist insurgency, and a humanitarian crisis.",
    "content": "The United Nations is concluding its political mission in Somalia on Saturday, marking the end of over 30 years of successive UN missions in the country. The closure comes as Somalia continues to grapple with political divisions, an ongoing Islamist insurgency, and a worsening humanitarian crisis.\n\nThe UN's presence in Somalia began in the early 1990s, initially as a humanitarian and peacekeeping operation. Over the years, the mission evolved into a political and peacebuilding effort, supporting state-building, constitutional processes, and elections. The current mission, known as UNSOM, was established in 2013 and has played a key role in advising the Somali government on peace and reconciliation.\n\nDespite progress in some areas, Somalia remains fragile. The militant group Al-Shabaab continues to carry out attacks, while political tensions between the federal government and regional states have hindered efforts to stabilize the country. The humanitarian situation has also deteriorated, with drought and conflict displacing millions.\n\nThe closure of the UN mission raises questions about the international community's future engagement in Somalia. The UN has said it will continue to provide humanitarian and development assistance through its agencies, but the political mission's exit leaves a gap in high-level mediation and coordination. Somali leaders have expressed mixed reactions, with some praising the mission's contributions and others calling for greater national ownership.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the end of the UN mission is significant. Many diaspora members have family in Somalia and have been actively involved in rebuilding efforts, from investing in businesses to supporting education and health initiatives. The UN's departure could affect the coordination of international support and the diaspora's ability to engage with multilateral processes. Diaspora organizations have urged continued international attention to Somalia's stability and development.\n\nAs the UN mission closes, the focus will be on how the Somali government and its international partners adapt to the new reality. The challenges facing the country remain immense, and the coming months will test the resilience of Somalia's institutions and the commitment of its partners.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/un-mission-closes-in-somalia-after-three-decades-as-political-tensions-persist/426961/",
