    "id": "news-1793351848234-0",
    "title": "Displaced Families from Baidoa Conflict Rely on Local Donations in Afgoye",
    "original_title": "Families fleeing deadly Baidoa conflict rely on small donations from locals in Afgoye",
    "slug": "displaced-families-from-baidoa-conflict-rely-on-local-donations-in-afgoye",
    "summary": "Families fleeing deadly conflict in Baidoa arrive in Afgoye with nothing, depending on small donations from locals. Maymun Mohamed Abdi recounts her narrow escape and the struggle to feed her five children.",
    "content": "Maymun Mohamed Abdi and her five children, the youngest just two years old, narrowly escaped the fighting in Baidoa when their home was struck by a missile. The assault began at nine in the evening and lasted until six the next morning, forcing the family to flee for their lives. They had never been displaced before, and now find themselves in a makeshift camp in Afgoye, about 90 kilometers southwest of Mogadishu, where they survive on handouts from local residents.\n\n\"We all survived, but a shell destroyed my house,\" Maymun told Radio Ergo. \"When the sun rose, I begged a truck driver to take us with him. We arrived here the following night.\" The family's ordeal reflects the broader toll of renewed clashes in Baidoa, the capital of South West State, which has seen repeated outbreaks of violence between rival armed groups in recent months. The fighting has forced thousands to abandon their homes, many for the first time.\n\nIn Afgoye, the new arrivals face dire conditions. Maymun's five children often go hungry, and the family relies on small donations from locals who themselves struggle to make ends meet. The camp lacks basic services such as clean water, healthcare, and education—challenges that compound the trauma of displacement. According to local aid workers, the number of displaced families in the area has swelled in recent weeks, straining already limited resources.\n\nBaidoa has long been a flashpoint in Somalia's conflict, with control contested by government forces, allied militias, and Al-Shabaab insurgents. The latest wave of violence has been particularly deadly, with reports of heavy civilian casualties. Many families have fled to Afgoye and other towns along the Shebelle River, seeking safety and assistance from relatives and humanitarian agencies. However, access to aid remains limited, and the needs are immense.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the plight of these displaced families is a stark reminder of the fragility of peace in their homeland. Many in the diaspora have relatives in Baidoa and surrounding areas, and they often serve as a lifeline through remittances. The crisis also underscores the importance of sustained support for local humanitarian efforts, as international funding struggles to keep pace with growing needs. Diaspora organizations and individuals can play a crucial role in providing emergency relief and supporting long-term recovery.\n\nMaymun's story is one of many, but it highlights the resilience and desperation of those caught in the crossfire. As the fighting continues, more families are expected to flee, adding to the already immense challenges facing communities in Afgoye and beyond. For now, Maymun and her children cling to the hope that peace will return to Baidoa so they can rebuild their lives.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/families-fleeing-deadly-baidoa-conflict-rely-on-small-donations-from-locals-in-afgoye/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
    "source_name": "Radio Ergo",
    "original_url": "https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/families-fleeing-deadly-baidoa-conflict-rely-on-small-donations-from-locals-in-afgoye/",
    "country": "Somalia",
