    "id": "news-1793297008860-0",
    "title": "African Union pushes media and civic engagement in democracy drive",
    "original_title": "AU advances drive to make democracy a lived experience across Africa, with media and socialisation at its core",
    "slug": "african-union-pushes-media-and-civic-engagement-in-democracy-drive",
    "summary": "The African Union Commission convened a two-day roundtable in Addis Ababa focused on media, socialisation and public participation as tools for strengthening democratic governance across the continent.",
    "content": "The African Union Commission's Department of Political Affairs, Peace and Security held the 2nd Roundtable on Democracy, Media and Socialisation in Africa in Addis Ababa on 26–27 October, according to Radio Dalsan. The meeting was organised under the theme \"From Dialogue to Action: Advancing Democratic Resilience through Media, Socialisation and Inclusion in Africa.\" Participants discussed ways to make democratic governance a practical, everyday reality for citizens rather than a set of formal institutions alone.\n\nThe roundtable is the second in a series of AU gatherings examining how media and civic socialisation can support democratic life. The Commission has framed the effort around people-centred approaches, credible information and public participation, with media cast as a central actor in that process. The first roundtable established a dialogue among stakeholders; this second meeting was positioned as a step toward concrete action.\n\nThe discussions come as the African Union has increasingly emphasised governance and democratic resilience as part of its broader peace and security agenda. AU bodies have argued that durable stability depends not only on elections but on inclusive civic engagement, access to reliable information and protections for public participation. The Addis Ababa meeting brought together participants from across the continent to examine how these principles can be operationalised in national contexts.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the agenda touches on issues that resonate closely with home-country debates. Somalia has been navigating a lengthy process of state-building and constitutional review, and questions of media freedom, credible information and inclusive political participation remain central to those discussions. Diaspora communities often serve as key sources of funding, commentary and civic organising, and many follow AU governance initiatives as signals of continental priorities that may shape policy at home.\n\nThe roundtable's emphasis on media and socialisation also reflects a wider concern among African institutions about misinformation and the role of independent journalism in democratic processes. How the AU translates this dialogue into practical support for member states — including through training, frameworks or monitoring — will be watched by analysts and civil society groups across the region.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/10/29/au-advances-drive-to-make-democracy-a-lived-experience-across-africa-with-media-and-socialisation-at-its-core/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=au-advances-drive-to-make-democracy-a-lived-experience-across-africa-with-media-and-socialisation-at-its-core",
    "source": "radiodalsan.com",
