    "slug": "somali-army-suffers-heavy-losses-in-al-shabaab-attack-inquiry-launched",
    "summary": "An estimated 2,000 troops advancing on Burhakabo were attacked by Al-Shabaab in Jame'o, leading to significant casualties and an inquiry into the military's worst reversal in years.",
    "content": "Somali military authorities have initiated an investigation following a deadly assault by Al-Shabaab on Monday that resulted in a rising number of casualties among government troops. The attack occurred as an estimated 2,000 soldiers were moving toward the town of Burhakabo in the country's southwest, according to security sources.\n\nThe troops were en route to Burhakabo, which forces loyal to former Southwest State president Abdiasis Laftagareen had seized several weeks prior. The militants struck in the village of Jame'o, situated on the road between Burhakabo and Leego. The insurgents reportedly employed suicide car bombs, causing heavy losses and forcing the soldiers to retreat in disarray.\n\nThe incident marks the military's most significant setback in recent years, prompting the authorities to open an inquiry into the events leading to the rout. The exact number of casualties remains unclear, but officials acknowledge the toll has continued to climb since Monday.\n\nThis event underscores the persistent security challenges in Somalia, where Al-Shabaab maintains a capacity to carry out sophisticated attacks despite ongoing military operations. The Somali diaspora, which closely follows developments back home, has expressed concern over the stability of the region and the effectiveness of the armed forces.\n\nFor the diaspora, the attack highlights the fragility of security gains and the potential impact on efforts to rebuild and invest in the country. Many in the diaspora have relatives serving in the security forces or living in affected areas, and they rely on accurate and timely information to assess the situation.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somali-army-toll-al-shabaab-assault-inquiry/",
